  "subtitle": "The first motion-sensitive game controller: a base-less wand that steers with a mercury core shifting in your hand",
  "why_scout_cares": "Le Stick is the museum's only fluid-orientation input device. Before the Wii by a quarter century, DataSoft sold a base-less pistol-grip joystick whose direction was read by a mercury core: tilt the wand and the liquid metal shifts, closing the switch for the corresponding cardinal direction. There is no stick shaft, no potentiometer, no microswitch lever — the input quantity is the physical orientation of your whole hand, decoded by gravity moving a drop of mercury. It is mechanically powered, needing no electricity. Released two years before the video-game crash, it failed because no game was designed for it, yet it remains the genuine first motion-sensitive game controller. Fills a distinct, empty physical-principle niche in an embodied-input-heavy collection.",
  "search_terms": ["Le Stick", "Datasoft Le Stick", "motion sensitive joystick 1981", "mercury joystick", "first motion controller"],
